WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.560 --> 00:00:04.000
There’s no shortage of theories and&nbsp;
questions concerning the incredibly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:04.000 --> 00:00:08.720
popular “Predator” films. From the alien's&nbsp;
true nature to the future of the franchise,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:08.720 --> 00:00:12.240
here are facts about the “Predator”&nbsp;
series that aren't really facts at all.

00:00:13.120 --> 00:00:17.600
To begin with, one thing to know about Predators&nbsp;
is that they're not really predators. It sounds&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:17.600 --> 00:00:22.400
confusing, but it's true. It's even pointed&nbsp;
out directly by the biologist Casey Bracket,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:22.400 --> 00:00:24.800
played by Olivia Munn, in "The Predator."

00:00:24.800 --> 00:00:26.160
"It hunts people for sport."

00:00:26.160 --> 00:00:28.000
"Technically, that's not a predator."

00:00:29.040 --> 00:00:30.160
"Thank you."

00:00:30.160 --> 00:00:34.560
After all, while "Predator" is a great name&nbsp;
for a movie about an alien who comes to Earth&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:34.560 --> 00:00:39.280
just to kill people, that doesn't mean it's&nbsp;
technically accurate as a label for the creature.

00:00:39.280 --> 00:00:44.400
In the natural world, a predator is a creature&nbsp;
that kills another organism for food. That last&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:44.400 --> 00:00:49.760
bit is important. Predators, as in actual Earth&nbsp;
animals in nature, kill because they need to eat.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:49.760 --> 00:00:53.760
In contrast, the Predators who appear&nbsp;
in the sci-fi franchise of the same name&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:53.760 --> 00:00:58.400
kill for sport or to prove their own prowess&nbsp;
as hunters, something that appears to be&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:58.400 --> 00:01:03.040
highly valued in their culture. The Predators&nbsp;
don't eat their kills — they just skin them,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:03.040 --> 00:01:08.160
hang them upside down, and sometimes remove their&nbsp;
skulls and spines to wave around triumphantly.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:08.160 --> 00:01:12.560
They don't want meat; they want trophies.&nbsp;
By definition, they're not predators.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:12.560 --> 00:01:16.000
That said, for the sake of clarity,&nbsp;
we're still going to call them Predators.

00:01:16.000 --> 00:01:18.080
If you haven't seen the "Predator"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:18.080 --> 00:01:21.920
movies — or if you just haven't paid that much&nbsp;
attention — it makes sense to believe that there's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:21.920 --> 00:01:27.040
one primary Predator throughout the franchise.&nbsp;
After all, the titles for every film except one,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:27.040 --> 00:01:33.200
2010's "Predators," are singular. There's a&nbsp;
Predator. And in 2018, he was "The Predator."

00:01:33.200 --> 00:01:36.640
Of course, none of that is true. The&nbsp;
main Predator, when there is one,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:36.640 --> 00:01:42.640
dies in literally every movie. The title creature&nbsp;
of 1987's "Predator" blows himself up with a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:42.640 --> 00:01:47.600
self-destruct device after being grievously&nbsp;
wounded by Dutch Schaefer, played by Arnold&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:47.600 --> 00:01:52.080
Schwarzenegger. The urban hunter of "Predator&nbsp;
2" is killed in battle with Mike Harrigan,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:52.080 --> 00:01:57.200
played by Danny Glover. All of the Predators in&nbsp;
"Predators" are taken down by their intended prey.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:57.200 --> 00:02:01.280
And the oversized, genetically hybridized&nbsp;
creature at the center of "The Predator"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:01.280 --> 00:02:04.560
is taken down by that movie's&nbsp;
central characters as well.

00:02:04.560 --> 00:02:08.451
The thing about Predators, of course, is that there are always more of them.

00:02:08.451 --> 00:02:12.051
Furthermore, it's pretty clear at this point that&nbsp;
hunting humans, presumably alongside&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:12.051 --> 00:02:16.771
other sentient creatures from around the galaxy,&nbsp;
is a cherished tradition on their planet,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:16.771 --> 00:02:20.211
so they're just going to keep doing&nbsp;
it, no matter how many die trying.

00:02:21.171 --> 00:02:24.051
Particularly with the first and&nbsp;
most beloved film in the series,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:24.051 --> 00:02:28.211
some of the franchise's most iconic imagery&nbsp;
comes from the Predator's point of view,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:28.211 --> 00:02:32.611
showing Schwarzenegger and other cast members&nbsp;
in bright colors that represent the intensity&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:32.611 --> 00:02:35.811
of their body heat as the creature&nbsp;
hunts them through the dense jungle.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:35.811 --> 00:02:40.371
This has led some viewers to mistakenly think&nbsp;
that the Predator species has actually evolved&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:40.371 --> 00:02:44.611
to see with thermal imaging. If you pay&nbsp;
attention, however, it becomes pretty clear&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:44.611 --> 00:02:49.651
that this infrared imaging is technological and&nbsp;
contained in the lenses of the Predators' masks.

00:02:49.651 --> 00:02:54.371
In "Predator 2," the extraterrestrial hunter&nbsp;
has multiple vision modes he can switch between.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:54.371 --> 00:02:57.091
And while we never see that&nbsp;
happen in the original film,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:57.091 --> 00:03:01.651
it's hard to deny that the otherworldly villain&nbsp;
here must have other forms of vision as well.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:01.651 --> 00:03:06.291
For example, how could he so easily avoid all&nbsp;
the traps set for him by the commandos if all&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:06.291 --> 00:03:10.851
he can see is heat. Plus, in the fantastic&nbsp;
scene where he first takes his mask off,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:10.851 --> 00:03:15.411
we watch as his vision completely changes,&nbsp;
with the infrared disappearing from sight.

00:03:15.411 --> 00:03:18.051
It's not just the Predators who die and are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:18.051 --> 00:03:23.251
replaced for every movie. Of the six "Predator"&nbsp;
films released to date, the two "AVP" movies,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:23.251 --> 00:03:28.051
not a single installment features a returning&nbsp;
human character from a previous movie. After&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:28.051 --> 00:03:32.451
more than three decades of that same formula, it&nbsp;
starts to look like none of the movies are really&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:32.451 --> 00:03:37.491
connected at all. The two most recent installments&nbsp;
in particular seem to bear the hallmarks of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:37.491 --> 00:03:43.011
franchise reboots. "Predators" – spoiler alert —&nbsp;
moves the action from Earth to an alien planet,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:43.011 --> 00:03:45.171
while "The Predator" has a new military-based&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:45.171 --> 00:03:49.091
story and a title distinguished from the&nbsp;
first movie only by the article "the."

00:03:49.731 --> 00:03:54.771
Of course, this isn't the case for either sci-fi&nbsp;
flick. Aside from the more complicated "AVP"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:54.771 --> 00:03:59.731
installments, each "Predator" movie establishes&nbsp;
that the previous films did indeed happen,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:59.731 --> 00:04:03.651
even if the events have been classified&nbsp;
so that most people don't know about them.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:03.651 --> 00:04:08.691
In "Predator 2," government agent Peter Keyes,&nbsp;
played by Gary Busey, discusses the events of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:08.691 --> 00:04:14.051
the first movie. In "Predators," the character of&nbsp;
Isabelle, played by Alice Braga, recognizes the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:14.051 --> 00:04:19.091
Predators from a description of the events of the&nbsp;
original "Predator." In "The Predator," the events&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:19.091 --> 00:04:23.971
of the first two movies are discussed and further&nbsp;
confirmed as real by the presence of Sean Keyes,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:23.971 --> 00:04:29.491
the son of Peter Keyes from "Predator 2,"&nbsp;
who's played by Gary Busey's actual son, Jake.

00:04:30.291 --> 00:04:34.131
Whatever character he's playing, Arnold&nbsp;
Schwarzenegger tends to be regarded as the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:34.131 --> 00:04:38.371
ultimate badass – and not without reason.&nbsp;
His godlike physique and apparent strength&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:38.371 --> 00:04:43.171
have placed him on a level above other&nbsp;
action heroes, especially in the 1980s.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:43.171 --> 00:04:47.491
It was a time when superhero physiques weren't&nbsp;
as widespread in movies as they've become in the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:47.491 --> 00:04:52.371
era of cinema today. So it makes perfect sense&nbsp;
when Schwarzenegger's Dutch survives the deaths&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:52.371 --> 00:04:57.251
of his teammates in "Predator" and has a final&nbsp;
showdown with the alien creature all on his own.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:57.251 --> 00:05:01.331
He's basically Earth's champion in that&nbsp;
moment, and he's fighting for us all.

00:05:01.331 --> 00:05:06.371
But contrary to what you might think, he's not the&nbsp;
only human to defeat a Predator in single combat.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:06.371 --> 00:05:11.651
In "Predator 2," Danny Glover's Mike Harrigan&nbsp;
single-handedly takes down a Predator of his own,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:11.651 --> 00:05:16.611
and his win might have been even more impressive.&nbsp;
The other Predators certainly seemed to think so.

00:05:16.611 --> 00:05:17.891
"You don’t know what you’re dealing with!"

00:05:18.691 --> 00:05:22.611
After the death of Peter Keyes, who thought&nbsp;
he had the Predator's number and was quickly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:22.611 --> 00:05:26.611
proven wrong, Harrigan and the titular&nbsp;
alien are left on their own. However,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:26.611 --> 00:05:31.811
Harrigan manages to sever one of the creature's&nbsp;
arms, robbing it of its self-destruct mechanism.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:31.811 --> 00:05:36.611
He then follows the Predator into an underground&nbsp;
spaceship and defeats it with its own blade.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:36.611 --> 00:05:41.091
Other Predators appear and carry off the dead&nbsp;
body, and their leader then honors Harrigan's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:41.091 --> 00:05:45.171
prowess by giving him an antique gun that&nbsp;
it had apparently taken on a previous hunt.

00:05:45.171 --> 00:05:47.971
Few sequels are as commonly maligned as&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:47.971 --> 00:05:52.931
"Predator 2." It came in fourth at the box office&nbsp;
on its opening weekend, was critically panned,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:52.931 --> 00:05:57.491
and later installments in the franchise were&nbsp;
celebrated for ignoring it. It's not a classic&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:57.491 --> 00:06:01.571
like its predecessor, and there's a good chance&nbsp;
you haven't even seen it. If that's the case,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:01.571 --> 00:06:05.571
go watch "Predator 2" and decide for yourself,&nbsp;
because it actually has a lot going for it.

00:06:05.571 --> 00:06:09.971
It's a clear and deliberate departure from the&nbsp;
first movie, but that's a better choice than&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:09.971 --> 00:06:14.931
trying to duplicate the original. "Predator" had&nbsp;
jungle action pretty well-covered, so "Predator&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:14.931 --> 00:06:19.411
2" brought its titular alien hunter to the&nbsp;
streets of downtown Los Angeles. What's more,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:19.411 --> 00:06:24.291
it's a near-future Los Angeles, in which gangs&nbsp;
rule the city, cops barely stand a chance,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:24.291 --> 00:06:28.211
and everybody's guns still shoot bullets&nbsp;
but they're weirdly big and chunky-looking.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:28.211 --> 00:06:32.691
The aforementioned Danny Glover is the hero,&nbsp;
as different from Arnold Schwarzenegger as an&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:32.691 --> 00:06:37.491
action star could be. In fact, it's ultimately&nbsp;
Glover's performance, as a detective who simply&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:37.491 --> 00:06:41.971
won't quit no matter what he's up against, that&nbsp;
makes "Predator 2" something of an underrated gem.

00:06:42.931 --> 00:06:48.131
In "Predator," the captured rebel, Anna, played&nbsp;
by Elpidia Carrillo, is the only survivor besides&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:48.131 --> 00:06:53.491
Schwarzenegger's Dutch. In "Predator 2," the&nbsp;
hunter corners Detective Leona Cantrell and then&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:53.491 --> 00:06:58.771
decides not to kill her. It also spares an unnamed&nbsp;
woman at the site of a massacre in a drug lord's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:58.771 --> 00:07:04.131
penthouse. In "Alien vs. Predator," the central&nbsp;
Predator actually befriends Lex Woods, played&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:04.131 --> 00:07:09.091
by Sanaa Lathan, and works with her to defeat&nbsp;
the aliens. Isabelle survives "Predators," and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:09.091 --> 00:07:14.611
Casey Bracket survives "The Predator." So what's&nbsp;
the deal? Do Predators not kill women? The truth&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:14.611 --> 00:07:18.931
is that, in theory, they might, but these movies&nbsp;
don't really have enough women for them to kill.

00:07:18.931 --> 00:07:21.811
Predators — at least traditional Predators&nbsp;
— don't necessarily care about gender.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:21.811 --> 00:07:26.371
They just don't kill unarmed people. It also&nbsp;
seems that they don't kill pregnant people,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:26.371 --> 00:07:31.011
which is why Detective Cantrell is spared.&nbsp;
The drug lord's girlfriend also falls under&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:31.011 --> 00:07:35.251
the unarmed rule. There’s also very few&nbsp;
female action heroes in these movies,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:35.251 --> 00:07:38.371
so it would've been a bummer for&nbsp;
audiences to see them slaughtered.

00:07:38.371 --> 00:07:42.691
Anna and Isabelle are literally the only&nbsp;
female characters in their respective films,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:42.691 --> 00:07:47.251
and Cantrell is just about the only woman in&nbsp;
"Predator 2" who's not a drug lord's unclothed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:47.251 --> 00:07:51.251
girlfriend. So killing them would've just&nbsp;
seemed really dark. At the end of the day,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:51.251 --> 00:07:55.731
what also keeps the Predators from killing&nbsp;
women is the narrative choices of these movies.

00:07:55.731 --> 00:07:59.011
As part of the gadget-filled techno-armor they&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:59.011 --> 00:08:03.571
prefer to wear when hunting humans, each Predator&nbsp;
has a metal face mask that they generally don't&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:03.571 --> 00:08:07.571
lose until the final showdown, when it's time to&nbsp;
give the heroes and the audience a good look at&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:07.571 --> 00:08:13.011
that creepy mandibled face. Because Predators show&nbsp;
up to Earth wearing this mask and don't usually&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:13.011 --> 00:08:17.411
take it off until shortly before they die, a lot&nbsp;
of people assume that the mask is what enables&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:17.411 --> 00:08:22.451
them to breathe in Earth's atmosphere. If you pay&nbsp;
attention, however, that's clearly not the case.

00:08:22.451 --> 00:08:26.771
The first Predator does without a mask for a&nbsp;
long time while fighting Dutch in the jungle,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:26.771 --> 00:08:31.411
and he seems to breathe just fine. In "Predator&nbsp;
2," the creature is seen breathing through another&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:31.411 --> 00:08:36.851
device after losing his mask, but we don't know&nbsp;
exactly why. Maybe Los Angeles' famous smog is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:36.851 --> 00:08:42.211
too much for him. This same movie establishes that&nbsp;
Predators breathe the same air as humans, however,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:42.211 --> 00:08:46.051
because Harrigan can breathe inside their&nbsp;
spaceship, even when interacting with an&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:46.051 --> 00:08:49.651
unmasked extraterrestrial. The same&nbsp;
thing is true of the alien planet in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:49.651 --> 00:08:54.371
"Predators" — the humans can breathe there&nbsp;
just fine, and so can the unmasked hunters.

00:08:54.371 --> 00:08:59.011
There's no doubt the mask provides copious&nbsp;
tools to aid a Predator's survival — starting&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:59.011 --> 00:09:03.545
with that famous thermal vision — but a&nbsp;
breathing apparatus is not one of them.

00:09:03.571 --> 00:09:08.451
Released in 2018, "The Predator" was only a&nbsp;
middling success at the box office, and it was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:08.451 --> 00:09:14.131
widely panned by critics. These days, however, it&nbsp;
takes a lot more than a bad installment or two to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:14.131 --> 00:09:19.251
kill a sci-fi action franchise. Like the Predators&nbsp;
themselves, the franchise just keeps getting up&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:19.251 --> 00:09:25.011
and coming for its prey: in this case, moviegoers.&nbsp;
In fact, the next movie is already in production.

00:09:25.011 --> 00:09:29.251
It's currently being called "Skulls" but&nbsp;
is likely to be retitled something with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:29.251 --> 00:09:33.971
the word "Predator" in it before release. The&nbsp;
upcoming installment will star Amber Midthunder&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:33.971 --> 00:09:38.691
of "Legion" as a Comanche woman who wants to be&nbsp;
a warrior and eventually encounters a Predator&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:38.691 --> 00:09:44.211
in the 19th century. "10 Cloverfield Lane"&nbsp;
director Dan Trachtenberg is helming the movie,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:44.211 --> 00:09:46.131
so it should at least be interesting.

00:09:46.131 --> 00:09:50.211
And if it doesn't work, that will be&nbsp;
disappointing, but it will unlikely be the end.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:50.211 --> 00:09:55.091
Surely, the Predators will keep returning their&nbsp;
favorite hunting grown: Earth's movie screens.

